#!/usr/bin/env bash
# pre-commit — refuse a commit that puts a ship path onto `main`.
#
# ASK GIT; DO NOT MODEL GIT. This replaces a commit check that lived in the Bash PreToolUse hook and tried
# to PREDICT, from the text of a command, whether a commit was about to happen and what it would contain.
# Five review rounds each found another form that parser got wrong: an option value that looked like a
# flag, `--dry-run`, a bare positional pathspec, `-S` swallowing `-a`, `git -C.`, `env FOO=1 git`, a bare
# `&`, a newline. Those were not eight defects. They were one — git's option grammar is not ours.
#
# Git runs THIS at the moment the answer exists, so `git diff --cached` is the real staged set rather than
# a guess at one. `git commit -a`, an explicit `--` pathspec, a bare positional pathspec,
# `--pathspec-from-file`, `--interactive`, `--amend` and a user alias all arrive here identically.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT THE COMPLETE CHECK, AND IT IS NOT TRYING TO BE. It fires on an event that creates a
# commit, so a merge, `git am`, a rebase, a fast-forward, a reset and an `update-ref` all move `main`
# without it running. Three earlier rounds tried to cover those by adding more commit-event hooks —
# `pre-merge-commit`, `pre-applypatch`, a `--no-ff` merge setting — and each round found the next event
# that creates no commit. `reference-transaction` beside this file guards the REF instead, which is the
# thing all of them were proxies for. Both of those hooks and that setting were deleted once it existed.
#
# So why keep this file at all: it refuses BEFORE the commit object is written and it names the staged
# files, which is the clearer error on the route a session actually takes every day. The ref hook is the
# backstop; this is the good message on the common path.
#
# ARMED BY ONE local setting, which every clone must run because the hook FILES are tracked and git does
# not arm them:
#
#   git config core.hooksPath scripts/githooks
#
# It used to take two, the second forcing a merge commit so a fast-forward could not slip past. The ref
# hook makes that unnecessary: it sees the fast-forward itself. test-hooks.sh asserts this checkout is
# armed, so an unarmed clone is a red control rather than a quiet absence.
#
# `--no-verify` skips this, as it skips the applicable verification hooks. That is
# GR-NEVER-WEAKEN-GUARDRAILS, not a hole to parse around: the Bash guard denies that token.
#
# EXIT: 0 allow · 1 this commit is refused · 2 the check itself broke.
# It FAILS CLOSED, unlike the PreToolUse guards, and the difference is deliberate. A broken PreToolUse hook
# that blocked every command would stop all work, so those fail open. This one runs at a single moment, on
# one commit, and refusing a commit it cannot judge costs nothing but a retry. `reference-transaction`
# behind it fails OPEN for the opposite reason: it runs inside `fetch` and `pull`, where failing closed
# would break the repository rather than protect one branch.

set -uo pipefail

fail() { echo "pre-commit check FAILED: $1" >&2; echo "This is not an approval. Fix the check." >&2; exit 2; }

ROOT=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || fail "could not resolve the repository root"
cd "$ROOT" || fail "could not enter the repository root"

BRANCH=$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null) || fail "could not resolve the branch"
[ "$BRANCH" = "main" ] || exit 0                  # the branch IS the protection

# `D` for a DELETION, which `ACMRT` omitted — removing a ship path from `main` is a ship-path commit.
# `--no-renames` so a rename is reported as both its old and new path, and a ship path renamed to a docs
# path cannot disappear into a single destination entry.
STAGED=$(git diff --cached --name-only --no-renames --diff-filter=ACDMRT) || fail "could not read the staged set"
[ -n "$STAGED" ] || exit 0

SHIP=$(printf '%s\n' "$STAGED" | python3 -c '
import sys, os
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join("scripts", "hooks"))
from ship_paths import is_ship_path
for line in sys.stdin:
    path = line.strip()
    if path and is_ship_path(path):
        print(path)
') || fail "could not classify the staged paths against scripts/hooks/ship_paths.py"
[ -n "$SHIP" ] || exit 0

COUNT=$(printf '%s\n' "$SHIP" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
echo "BLOCKED: committing $COUNT ship-path file(s) on \`main\`." >&2
printf '%s\n' "$SHIP" | head -8 | sed 's/^/    /' >&2
[ "$COUNT" -gt 8 ] && echo "    ... and $((COUNT - 8)) more" >&2
cat >&2 <<'MSG'

Ship-path work goes on a branch, per .claude/rules/workflow-process.md RULE: ten-step-shape.
Measured 2026-08-29: a Codex-cleared change to the insertion path sat in 23 uncommitted files
on `main` for six hours with that rule in context.

  git checkout -b <type>/<issue>-<slug>   # your staged set follows you
MSG
exit 1
